.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt WATTLE-DEPENDENCIES 7
.Os Wattle DEVEL
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm wattle-dependencies
.Nd dependencies of Wattle projects
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A
.Em dependency
is code from another project that a Wattle project uses.
A project may include a copy of that code in its source tree
or declare a remote library in its info file.
A copy included in the source tree is
.Em vendored
and committed with the project.
Remote libraries are fetched into
.Eo
.Pa .wattle/
.Ec
and are not committed.
.Pp
Installing an application does not make its modules available
to other projects.
Dependencies are obtained for each project.
See
.Xr wattle-projects 7
for project layout and artifact declarations.
.
.Sh VENDORING\c
.Em Vendoring
is the practice of copying a dependency into the project’s
source tree.
The copy becomes part of the project and may be edited by its
author.
Running the project does not require the vendored source to
be fetched again.
.Pp
Each project uses its own copy.
Changes to the original dependency do not affect that copy;
the project author must update it to include a fix or a new
version.
.Pp
Vendored source is not added to the module path.
It is imported by a relative path,
for example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpimport \&./deps/greetings\*(rp
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Ql ":vendored"
key in the info file contains a vector of source declarations.
.Ql "wattle deps vendor"
copies the specified files into the project,
replacing files that already exist.
Each declaration is a map with these keys:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xo
.Ql ":name"
.Xc
A non-empty string identifying the source.
Every declaration in
.Ql ":vendored"
must have a different name.
.It Xo
.Ql ":url"
.Xc
A source directory,
tarball URL ending in
.Ql "\&.tar"
or
.Ql "\&.tar\&.gz" ,
or Git repository,
as for
.Ql "wattle apps install" .
.It Xo
.Ql ":ref"
.Xc
An optional branch or tag for a Git repository.
Without it,
each invocation uses HEAD of the default branch.
.It Xo
.Ql ":prefix"
.Xc
The destination directory,
relative to the project.
The default is the current directory.
.It Xo
.Ql ":paths"
.Xc
An optional vector of paths to copy.
Each entry is a string or a vector of two strings.
A string specifies the same path in the source and under
.Ql ":prefix" .
A pair specifies the source path followed by the destination
path under
.Ql ":prefix" .
Without
.Ql ":paths" ,
the whole source is copied.
.El
.Pp
Paths cannot be absolute or contain
.Ql "\&.\&." .
All entries are checked before any source is fetched.
Vendoring records no source revision.
The committed copies determine the version used by the project.
.Pp
A vendored source needs no
.Ql "info\&.edn"
and no library declaration.
.Ql ":for"
does not affect vendoring.
For installation of a source executable,
include its vendored files in the executable’s
.Ql ":files" .
.
.Sh REMOTE LIBRARIES
A
.Em remote library
is a library fetched from another project.
Its declaration is a map in
.Ql ":libs"
with
.Ql ":type :remote" .
The
.Ql ":url"
key specifies a Git repository or a tarball URL ending in
.Ql "\&.tar"
or
.Ql "\&.tar\&.gz" .
The
.Ql ":hash"
key specifies the exact source revision to use.
.
.Ss Git repositories
For a Git repository,
.Ql ":hash"
is a commit hash of 40 lowercase hexadecimal digits,
or 64 for a SHA-256 repository.
Fetching first requests that commit alone at depth 1.
If the server does not allow a fetch by hash,
Wattle clones the repository instead.
Fetching fails if the commit cannot be found.
.Pp
The optional
.Ql ":ref"
specifies a branch or tag for the clone.
It is needed when the default branch does not reach the pinned
commit.
Otherwise it only labels the commit;
it does not select a different revision.
.
.Ss Tarballs
For a tarball,
.Ql ":hash"
is
.Ql "sha256:"
followed by 64 lowercase hexadecimal digits.
This is the tree hash of the extracted files,
as application installation records it under
.Ql ":rev" ,
rather than a hash of the archive.
A tarball declaration cannot have
.Ql ":ref" .
.
.Ss Fetching
If
.Ql ":hash"
is missing,
fetching reports an error with the hash of the fetched source.
Add that hash to the declaration to pin the revision.
A library is fetched again only when its
.Ql ":url"
or
.Ql ":hash"
changes.
.Pp
.Ql "wattle build lib"
fetches and places remote libraries on the module path.
.Ql "wattle build exe"
also fetches and places those associated with the native executables
it builds.
Running a script does not fetch libraries.
.Pp
The fetched source and its revision record are saved in
.Eo
.Pa .wattle/.remote/<name>/
.Ec .
A source library is copied to
.Eo
.Pa .wattle/<name>.wattle
.Ec
or
.Eo
.Pa .wattle/<name>/
.Ec ,
depending on whether its root is a file or directory.
A native library is built in
.Eo
.Pa zig-out/lib/
.Ec .
Programs import the library by the name in its declaration:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\*(lpimport greetings\*(rp
.Ed
.Pp
A remote library requires a matching library declaration in the
source project.
Source without an info file must be vendored instead.
The
.Ql ":for"
of the local declaration associates the library with the project’s
executables.
Installation fetches associated remote libraries separately;
see
.Xr wattle-applications 7 .
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Vendor selected files into
.Eo
.Pa deps/greetings/
.Ec :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{:vendored \(lB{:name \(dqgreetings\(dq
             :url \(dqhttps://example\&.org/greetings\&.git\(dq
             :ref \(dqv1\&.0\&.0\(dq
             :prefix \(dqdeps/greetings\(dq
             :paths \(lB\(lB\(dqsrc/init\&.wattle\(dq \(dqinit\&.wattle\(dq\(rB \(dqLICENSE\(dq\(rB}\(rB}
.Ed
.Pp
Copy the source:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ wattle deps vendor
.Ed
.Pp
Declare a remote library for a native executable:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{:exes \(lB{:type :native :name \(dqhello\(dq :entry \(dqmain\&.wattle\(dq}\(rB
 :libs \(lB{:type :remote
         :name \(dqgreetings\(dq
         :url \(dqhttps://example\&.org/greetings\&.git\(dq
         :hash \(dq0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567\(dq
         :for \(lB\(dqhello\(dq\(rB}\(rB}
.Ed
.Pp
The hash must identify a commit in the source repository.
A branch or tag in
.Ql ":ref"
does not replace it.
.
